<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Beatrice I, Countess of Burgundy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1143–<a href="/years/1184">1184</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anscarids">Anscarids</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Beatrice I was countess of the Free County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> from 1148, the only child of Count Renaud III. Her marriage in 1156 to Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a> tied Burgundy to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a>, and she was crowned empress in 1167 and queen of Burgundy in 1178. Her children included the emperor Henry VI and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-i-duke-of-bavaria">Otto I</a>, Count of Burgundy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/beatrice-i-countess-of-burgundy.jpg" alt="Beatrice I, Countess of Burgundy" width="210" height="244" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BeatrixBarbarossy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Beatrice was the only surviving child of Reginald III, Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, and Agatha of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>, and was named after her grandmother. When her father died in 1148 she inherited the County of Burgundy and became countess palatine, which made her one of the most desired brides in the Empire. Her uncle William III of Macon, acting as her regent, tried to deprive her of her rights and had her imprisoned until Emperor Frederick I stopped him by proposing a marriage. The emperor may have been attracted by an alternative to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brenner-pass">Brenner Pass</a>, a strategic position against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> and Burgundian knights for his wars. They married at Wurzburg on 9 June 1156.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Frederick took control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> as co-ruler by right of his wife, though Beatrice's name seldom appears in its charters before 1166. She accompanied him on campaigns, and in July 1159 she brought reinforcements of 1,200 knights from Burgundy to the Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crema">Crema</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> was destroyed in 1162, the Annals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> say that at her suggestion the lives and property of the Milanese were spared. She was crowned empress in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> by the antipope Paschal III on 1 August 1167. On the retreat, a plague struck the army and she was attacked at Pontremoli, escaping the arrows, and then stayed in hostile Susa until 1168.</p><p>After the Peace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> of 1177 her imperial coronation, performed by an antipope, was declared void, and she was crowned queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienne">Vienne</a> on 15 August 1178. She then seems to have governed Burgundy by herself, issuing nine charters of her own between 1181 and her death, many witnessed by her younger son Otto, her designated heir. She still called herself empress in them. Contemporaries called her a litterata, learned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>. The early minnesang is associated with the troubadours she brought to court, including Guyot de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/provins">Provins</a>, and Gautier d'<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arras">Arras</a> dedicated a version of his Ille et Galeron to her.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1184 Beatrice fell ill with an unknown illness at Jouhe and died quickly, aged about 40. She was buried in Speyer Cathedral and her heart in the old Benedictine abbey at Jouhe. Frederick grieved. They had eleven children, among them the emperor Henry VI, Philip, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> from 1198, and Otto I, Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>. Sources for her political activity are scarce compared with those for earlier empresses, but recent research holds her role was more important than once estimated: contemporaries sought to influence the emperor through her, and she intervened for people connected to her and for churches, monasteries and hospitals.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Beatrice I of Burgundy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Frederick Barbarossa’s marriage to <b>Beatrice of Burgundy</b> in 1156 and his coronation at Arles in 1178 renewed imperial claims, and the archbishop of Trier bore the title of arch-chancellor for the kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-arles">Kingdom of Arles · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Countess <b>Beatrice</b> married Frederick Barbarossa in 1156, tying the county to the Hohenstaufen.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-county-of-burgundy">Free County of Burgundy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Château de Dole, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Jouhe, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.09,5.49&amp;year=1169" aria-label="Château de Dole on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4679 511 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Beatrice I, Countess of Burgundy"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5129 781h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5129 781h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.09,5.49&amp;year=1169">What was here in 1169? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
